### uv

uv is a Python package and project manager written in Rust. One binary does the work that several older tools used to split between them:

- **Python version installation**: downloads and pins a specific Python version per project (replaces `pyenv`).
- **Virtual environment creation**: creates the isolated Python environment that the project's dependencies live in (replaces `python -m venv`).
- **Dependency resolution**: figures out which package versions satisfy every requirement (replaces parts of `pip`).
- **Lockfile generation**: writes `uv.lock`, a file listing the exact versions installed. Two engineers running `uv sync` with the same lockfile get the same versions, even months apart.
- **Workspace coordination**: lets multiple Python packages in this repo share one set of dependencies and one virtual environment.

Installs and resolutions are an order of magnitude faster than the older pip/Poetry stack. The Google Python Buildpack uses uv natively for Python 3.14+, matching what runs locally.

### Ruff

Ruff is a Python linter and formatter written in Rust. A linter checks code style and common bug patterns; a formatter rewrites code to a consistent layout. Ruff implements the rules of older tools (black, isort, flake8, pycodestyle, pyflakes, most of pylint) plus a growing set of ruff-specific rules. Configuration lives in one section of `pyproject.toml`.

One tool replaces the four-or-five-tool legacy stack. Runtime is an order of magnitude faster, configuration is centralized, and the rule selection is documented inline in `pyproject.toml`. Tests are exempted from docstring and annotation strictness via `per-file-ignores`, since test code prioritizes readability over interface contracts.

### Pyright

Pyright is Microsoft's static type checker for Python. "Static" means it checks types by reading the code, without running it. Python is dynamically-typed, but type annotations (e.g. `def add(a: int, b: int) -> int:`) let tools like Pyright catch type-related bugs before runtime.

Strict mode requires annotations on every function signature, flags any value Pyright can't determine the type of (implicit `Any`), and rejects unsafe casts. It's the same type checker that VS Code's Pylance extension uses, so the diagnostics in your editor match what CI reports.

Strict mode catches a class of bugs that lighter checking misses: forgotten None handling, wrong return types, mis-typed dict keys. The annotation cost is small for new code and pays off at every refactor.

One override: `reportMissingTypeStubs` is downgraded from error to warning. Several `google-cloud-*` libraries ship incomplete stubs (the files that describe types for code Pyright can't read directly), and treating that as a CI-blocking error creates churn that isn't about code quality.

### pytest

pytest is the de facto standard Python test framework. Tests are plain Python functions whose names start with `test_`; pytest discovers and runs them. Fixtures (reusable setup) and parametrization (running one test with many inputs) come from decorators. `pytest-cov` reports test coverage during a test run.

Coverage source is `["src"]`, which auto-discovers package code under any `src/` directory in the workspace. New apps and libraries are picked up without enumerating them individually.

## Repo structure

### Monorepo

A monorepo is one git repository that holds multiple deployable units (apps) and shared libraries (libs), instead of giving each its own repo. uv's workspace feature makes this work cleanly: `apps/*` and `libs/*` are declared as workspace members in the root `pyproject.toml`, and they share one Python environment, one lockfile, and one dependency resolution.

When an app needs code from `libs/shared`, the app's `pyproject.toml` declares the dependency and uv resolves it to the local workspace path. A change in `libs/shared` is immediately visible to every app that uses it; there is no publish-and-bump cycle.

The workspace root has no `[project]` section, just `[tool.uv.workspace]` and `[dependency-groups]`. This is called a "virtual" workspace root: the directory coordinates members without being a Python package itself.

### src/ layout

The src layout puts package code under `<package>/src/<package_name>/` instead of `<package>/<package_name>/`. Tests live under `<package>/tests/`.

Here is why this matters. When Python runs a script, it adds the script's directory to the import path. Without the src layout, running `pytest` from a package's root directory makes Python find the in-tree source code (`<package>/<package_name>/`) before checking the installed virtual environment. Tests can then pass even when a packaging bug (missing `__init__.py`, missing files in `package_data`, etc.) would make the installed package broken.

With the src layout, the package source is under `src/`, which is not on the import path by default. Tests can only find the package through the installed virtual environment, the same way production code finds it. Packaging bugs surface immediately. This is the standard recommendation for any Python package today.

### apps, libs, infra, scripts

The repo is partitioned by what each directory holds:

- `apps/`: deployable services and jobs. Each subdirectory is a separate Python package and becomes a Cloud Run service or job.
- `libs/`: shared code, imported by apps. `libs/shared/` is the single shared library today; additional libraries can be extracted as patterns emerge.
- `infra/`: Terraform configuration for cloud resources.
- `scripts/`: operational shell scripts (`bootstrap.sh`, `new-app.sh`, `deploy.sh`, `tf-bootstrap.sh`, `restore-from-template.sh`).
- `app-template/`: scaffold copied by `make new-app`. Not deployed.

The split keeps Python application code separate from infrastructure code, deployment scripts, and the new-app scaffolding. Each has its own tooling lifecycle.

### Shared library design

`libs/shared/` is the single shared library today. Some decisions about it:

**When to move code from an app into `libs/shared`.** Move it when two or more apps need the same logic *and* that logic has stabilized. The key constraint is stabilized: moving code that is still being iterated creates coupling between apps at a surface that keeps changing. The right moment is when the shape is settled and the duplication cost has become concrete.

**When to create a second library.** When a block of shared code has a distinct concern and a different consumer set. A test utilities library, for example, is only needed by test code; putting it in `libs/shared` forces it onto every app's dependency tree. If the boundary is real, the library separation is warranted.

**One-way dependency rule.** Apps may depend on libs. Libs must not depend on apps. A lib that finds itself needing app-specific behavior is a signal that the abstraction is inverted — the logic belongs in the app, not the lib.

**No versioning.** Workspace libs update in-place. When a shared interface changes, update every caller in the same commit. The lockfile captures the snapshot; `git log` is the changelog. The publish-and-bump cycle that external packages require does not apply inside a workspace.

### app-template

`app-template/` is a directory tree containing the files a new app needs: a `pyproject.toml`, a `main.py`, a tests directory. The files contain `__APP_NAME__` placeholders where the app's name should go. `make new-app NAME=my-service` invokes `new-app.sh`, which copies the tree to `apps/my-service/` and replaces every `__APP_NAME__` with `my_service` (or whatever you named it) using `sed`.

Templating happens in two phases. First, copier substitutes Jinja variables (`{{ python_version }}`, `{{ project_name }}`, conditional content) once, when the project is first generated from the template. Second, `new-app.sh` substitutes `__APP_NAME__` each time you create a new app later. The two phases exist because copier's variables are out of scope by the time `new-app.sh` runs.

`app-template/` is excluded from ruff and pyright. The placeholder files there aren't valid Python until `new-app.sh` runs.

### copier

copier is a project templating tool. It generates new projects from a template by asking questions (Python version, framework, GCP or not, etc.) and rendering files based on the answers. The template repo for this project lives at the URL stored in `.copier-answers.yml`.

Unlike a plain "use this template" button on GitHub, copier supports updates: `copier update --trust` re-fetches the latest template version, re-renders with the previously-given answers, and merges changes into the existing project. This is how template improvements (new tooling, security fixes, CI changes) reach existing projects without manual file-copying.

Conditional content in the template lives in two forms: file-level exclusion via `_exclude` rules in `copier.yaml` (for whole files like Terraform when `include_terraform` is false), and inline Jinja conditionals within files (for things like the GCP-specific logging processors in `logging_setup.py`).

## Code patterns

### structlog

structlog is a structured logging library for Python. The standard library's `logging` module produces lines of free-form text; structlog produces lines of structured data (key-value pairs), which makes logs much easier to query and aggregate in tools like Cloud Logging.

A typical structlog call looks like `log.info("payment_processed", user_id=user.id, amount=amount, currency="USD")`. The output is a JSON object with those fields plus a timestamp and severity.

Logs go to stdout as JSON when the app runs in Cloud Run (detected via the `K_SERVICE` env var) and as colored console output to stderr in development. Cloud Run auto-ingests anything on stdout into Cloud Logging, so application code needs no `CloudLoggingHandler` or GCP client in the logging path. Field names follow Cloud Logging conventions: `severity` (uppercase string) is renamed from structlog's `level`, and `logging.googleapis.com/trace` is populated when a trace ID is present in the structlog context (typically set by HTTP middleware on the inbound request).

### Configuration and secrets

`BaseAppSettings` (in `libs/shared`) is a pydantic-settings model. pydantic-settings reads configuration from environment variables and `.env` files when the model is instantiated, with type validation: declare `port: int = 8080` and you get an integer back, with an error if the env var can't be parsed.

Secrets (database passwords, API keys, etc.) are read separately via `get_secret("SECRET_NAME")` from `shared.gcp`. This function looks up the named secret in Google Secret Manager and returns its value.

Two reasons for the split. First, type-safe config loading and secret retrieval are different operations with different failure modes; mixing them makes both harder to reason about. Second, explicit `get_secret` calls keep the data flow visible in code: a reader can tell which values come from environment variables (config) and which from Secret Manager (secrets), and exactly what code reads each.

{% if include_docker -%}
### Multi-stage Dockerfile

A Docker image holds everything needed to run an application: the Python interpreter, dependencies, application code, and system libraries. A multi-stage build splits the work into two stages so the final image is small and clean.

The builder stage installs dependencies into a virtual environment via `uv sync --frozen --no-dev --package <app>`. It uses a heavier base image with build tools, since this stage is discarded. The runtime stage starts fresh from a slim base image, copies only the virtual environment from the builder, copies the app code, and runs as a non-root user. The final image contains only what's needed at runtime.

The `--package <app>` flag is uv-specific. It installs only the named workspace member and its transitive dependencies; other workspace members are excluded. A 5-app workspace produces 5 small images, not 5 copies of a large one.

{% endif -%}
