## Process

### Conventional commits

Conventional commits is a specification for commit message format. Each commit message follows `<type>(<scope>): <subject>`, where `type` is one of a fixed set (`feat` for a new feature, `fix` for a bug fix, `chore` for routine maintenance, `docs` for documentation, `refactor` for non-functional changes, etc.) and `scope` optionally names the part of the code touched. Examples:

- `feat(auth): add OAuth login support`
- `fix(api): handle null user IDs in /users/{id}`
- `chore: update dependencies`

The `conventional-pre-commit` hook enforces this format on the commit message itself; commits that don't match are rejected locally before they reach the remote.

Consistent commit message format makes git history scannable and downstream automation tractable. Changelog generation and semantic-versioning automation become available later if needed. The format is mechanical and the cost is one-line learning. Signed commits (GPG) add cryptographic provenance at the cost of onboarding friction (key generation, configuration). Not configured by this template.

### Pre-commit

pre-commit is a tool that runs checks ("hooks") against your staged files automatically, every time you run `git commit`. If any hook fails, the commit is rejected and you fix the issues. Hooks are configured in `.pre-commit-config.yaml`. To enable them on your machine, run `pre-commit install` once (bootstrap.sh does this); it adds a git hook script that runs pre-commit before each commit.

The configured hooks are ruff (lint and format), pyright (type-check), and several file-hygiene checks (trailing whitespace, end-of-file newlines, YAML syntax). The principle: by the time code reaches review, no one should be commenting on formatting, import order, or unused imports. Reviewers focus on logic and design.

### CI

CI (continuous integration) runs in GitHub Actions: a set of workflows defined in `.github/workflows/` that GitHub runs on certain events. `ci.yml` runs lint, format-check, typecheck, and tests on every pull request and on pushes to `develop` and `main`. `deploy.yml` builds the container image, pushes it to Artifact Registry, and deploys to Cloud Run — to `dev` on push to `develop`, to `prod` on push to `main`.
{% if include_terraform -%}
Two Terraform workflows when applicable: `terraform-plan.yml` shows the planned infrastructure changes on PRs that touch `infra/`, and `terraform-apply.yml` applies changes — to `dev` on push to `develop`, to `prod` on push to `main`.
{% endif %}
CI invokes `make` targets rather than raw commands. Local `make ci` and CI run the same checks. Drift between them is hard to introduce.

{% if use_gcp -%}
### Deployment

This project uses Git Flow. Two long-lived branches map directly to two environments:

- `develop` is the integration branch. Feature branches merge here via PR. Pushing to `develop` automatically deploys to `dev`. New code reaches a real GCP environment within minutes, where it can be tested against real services without affecting customers.
- `main` is the production branch. When `develop` is ready for a release, it is merged to `main` via PR. Pushing to `main` triggers a deployment to `prod`.

Prod deploys are gated. The `prod` GitHub environment has a required-reviewers protection rule, so even an automated push to `main` waits for explicit human approval before the deployment runs.

This split keeps the feedback loop fast in dev (commit, push, see it running) while keeping prod behind a deliberate decision.
{% if include_terraform -%}
The same pattern applies to Terraform: `terraform apply` runs automatically on push to `develop` for dev, and on push to `main` for prod (gated by the `prod` environment's required-reviewers rule).
{% endif %}
